// Who this build is signed as, and what it calls itself.
//
// Both are per-machine facts, not project ones: they name a particular Apple developer
// account and a bundle id registered against it. Anyone else building this repo does so
// under their own developer team, so neither value can live in a committed file without
// each push reassigning the other person's.
//
// Real values go in `Signing.local.xcconfig` next to this file, which is gitignored — see
// the `#include?` at the bottom. Nothing here is secret (a Team ID is visible in any
// distributed app), it just has no business being shared or fought over in a diff.
//
// Kept as a config file rather than in the Xcode UI because settings made through Xcode
// live in `project.pbxproj`, which XcodeGen rewrites from scratch on every `xcodegen
// generate` — so they silently vanish. XcodeGen never rewrites this file, it only
// references it.

// Empty is the correct default, not a made-up team id. Simulator builds need no team at
// all, so a fresh clone builds and tests without one; a device build fails with Xcode's
// own "requires a development team", which is a far better prompt than mysteriously
// signing against an account you don't have.
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =

// THE BUNDLE ID IS THE APP'S IDENTITY ON A PHONE, AND IDENTITY IS WHAT OWNS THE USER'S
// DATA. iOS keys a container to it: change it and the next install is a brand-new app with
// no parks and no visits, while the old container sits there under the old id, invisible
// and unreachable from the new one.
//
// It also names the iCloud container once CloudKit is enabled, so changing it after the
// friends features go live orphans the published profile and visit records too.
//
// This default is therefore a deliberate tripwire, not a usable value — see `Verify
// signing identity` in project.yml, which fails a device build while this value is still
// here rather than letting it install a second, empty ParkTrack alongside the real one.
//
// Simulator builds are left alone: no real data lives in a simulator, and a fresh clone
// must still be able to build and run the test suite with no local file at all.
PARKTRACK_BUNDLE_ID = com.example.CHANGE-ME.parktrack

// Which entitlements the app is signed with, as a variable so a build can go out without
// any.
//
// The entitlement names an iCloud container, and a container belongs to one team. So a build
// signed as a different team than the one that owns the container cannot carry this file at
// all — it fails to provision rather than quietly doing without. That case is real and not
// rare: rebuilding the *previous* bundle id under the *previous* team, to get a backup out of
// an app before retiring it, is exactly the move that carries a whole install across to a new
// developer account.
//
// Set to empty in Signing.local.xcconfig for such a build. The app is fully functional
// without it: `CloudKitAvailability` reports unavailable, friends fall back to sample data,
// and everything stays on the one device — which is all a build being used to export a backup
// needs to do.
PARKTRACK_ENTITLEMENTS = ParkTrack/ParkTrack.entitlements

// Which CloudKit environment the app talks to: `Development` or `Production`.
//
// The two are separate databases with separate schemas and no data between them. Xcode
// signs a device build for Development by default, and TestFlight and the App Store always
// get Production regardless of what is written here — so without this, a build run from
// Xcode looks at an empty database while the phone next to it is full of real friends, and
// the only way to reproduce anything a tester reports is to upload a build and wait.
//
// Production is therefore the default: it is what every build that reaches anyone else
// uses anyway, and it makes an Xcode build and a TestFlight build the same app looking at
// the same data. Set this to `Development` in Signing.local.xcconfig when you want a
// sandbox to break — a schema change worth trying before deploying it, or anything
// destructive aimed at records other people can see.
//
// If automatic signing refuses ("provisioning profile doesn't support the
// com.apple.developer.icloud-container-environment entitlement"), the App ID has no
// production profile yet; archiving once, or a visit to the portal, creates it.
PARKTRACK_ICLOUD_ENV = Production

// Local overrides. `#include?` is the optional form — no error when the file is absent,
// which is the fresh-clone case. Last assignment wins in an xcconfig, so this has to come
// after the defaults above or it would be the one being overridden.
#include? "Signing.local.xcconfig"
